th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","history_only":true,"curation_sha256":"402117cd2ecc28eaf6387aaefcadec5f0c0540f0912f8a96983845daf37ec8e2","concept_id":"Year-2/2b-Risk-Insurance/exposure-offset.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-2/2b-Risk-Insurance/exposure-offset.html","questions":[{"id":"year_2_2b_risk_insurance_exposure_offset_c6fc90cd_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"An analyst replaces the fixed exposure offset by $\\gamma\\log e$ and fits $\\gamma=0.8$, so the count mean is $\\mathrm{e}^{0.8}\\lambda$ with exposure $e>0$ and fixed rate factor $\\lambda>0$. When exposure triples, what shows this differs from the proportional offset model?","options":["The count mean triples, so the fitted exposure effect remains proportional.","The count mean falls, because an exposure coefficient below one reverses the effect.","The count mean increases by a factor 3^0.8 rather than 3.","The count mean stays unchanged, because recorded exposure is a known quantity."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7c092d112635af51210fbbd0","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_2_2b_risk_insurance_exposure_offset_c6fc90cd_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"All records in an offset model have exactly one policy-year of exposure, and log rate is $\\eta$. Their known log-exposure offsets are therefore zero. Which statement correctly describes this design?","options":["A zero offset implies a zero Poisson mean in every record.","The rate cannot be identified because the known offset coefficient is not estimated.","Exposure can now be omitted for every future record regardless of its duration.","The count mean equals exp(eta), the per-policy-year rate, for these unit-exposure records."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c7c4dc40d36c50b5c2e8e784","time_limit":35,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_2_2b_risk_insurance_exposure_offset_c6fc90cd_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A Poisson offset record has exposure $e=1.25$ and claim rate $\\lambda=0.2$ in consistent units. What is the probability that it records zero claims?","options":["$\\mathrm{e}^{-0.25}$.","$\\mathrm{e}^{-0.2}$.","$\\mathrm{e}^{-1.25}$.","$1-\\mathrm{e}^{-0.25}$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"77d7db419c3ba05f29eb6485","time_limit":25,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"}]}}